    <!--
      notifications.v1 — the consent surface.

      Every string on the notification screens is here rather than inlined in
      Compose, because permission copy is a security control and has to be
      reviewable and translatable as one. Two rules bind what may be written
      here, both from the functional specification (01 §8.1):

        * nothing may offer Android's own OTP redaction as reassurance.
          POC-NOTIF-01 measured this exact device delivering six OTP-shaped
          notifications to an untrusted listener entirely unredacted, with the
          platform flag on and Google's classifier bound. "Android hides
          verification codes" would be false here;
        * nothing may imply OmniBridge inspects notification content. It does not,
          by design (ADR-0015 §5). The honest sentence is the one the product
          can keep: what you choose to share is shared in full with the
          computer you named.

      Note also what cannot be interpolated into any string below: a
      notification's title, body, or an application's own text. There is no
      format specifier here that could take one.
    -->
    <string name="notif_screen_title">Notifications</string>
    <string name="notif_capability_title">Notifications</string>
    <string name="notif_capability_description">Show this device\'s notifications on the computer</string>

